{"book":"Job","chapter":39,"evidence_availability":"AVAILABLE","failure_reason":null,"generated_metadata":{"commentary_prompt_version":"1.8","commentary_schema_version":"1.2","evidence_bundle_version":"1.1","evidence_hash":"b6a4fe935dbfee88252a230fdb331c777f25c141061a767fd83556a81c3f60a0","generated_timestamp":null,"model":"gpt-5.6-terra","renderer_label":"commentary-v1.2-codex-session","synthesis_compiler_version":"1.1","synthesis_hash":"d41c0aaf8b32cc716e27b2eadf60a9fb6ced9e2cd5484c022cf53c14dfeba57a","synthesis_schema_version":"1.1"},"reference":"Job 39","sections":[{"blocks":[{"confidence":"high","evidence_ids":["job-whirlwind-creation","job:interpretive_note:11","job:interpretive_note:7"],"id":"block_1","interpretation_level":"disputed","synthesis_ids":["syn_surrounding_passages_133a33d96d8b"],"text":"Job 39 belongs to the divine speeches, where YHWH answers Job through a long series of questions and descriptions of creation. This chapter’s wild animals fit that larger movement: the questions draw attention to creatures beyond human control or use. The speeches answer Job personally, but they do not disclose a simple cause for his suffering. Readers differ over whether this creation tour chiefly exposes Job’s limited knowledge, displays the value and freedom of nonhuman creatures, challenges Job’s proposed governance, or offers encounter rather than explanation; the speech need not be reduced to only one of these functions.","verse_refs":["Job 10:1-22","Job 11:1-20","Job 12:1-25","Job 13:1-28","Job 14:1-22","Job 15:1-35","Job 16:1-22","Job 17:1-16","Job 18:1-21","Job 19:1-29","Job 1:1-22","Job 20:1-29","Job 21:1-34","Job 22:1-30","Job 23:1-17","Job 24:1-25","Job 25:1-6","Job 26:1-14","Job 27:1-23","Job 28:1-28","Job 29:1-25","Job 2:1-13","Job 30:1-31","Job 31:1-40","Job 32:1-22","Job 33:1-33","Job 34:1-37","Job 35:1-16","Job 36:1-33","Job 37:1-24","Job 38:1-41","Job 3:1-26","Job 40:1-24","Job 41:1-34","Job 42:1-17","Job 4:1-21","Job 5:1-27","Job 6:1-30","Job 7: